LINING the base of the community’s disease-progression structure. [Data: Entities (2650, 2666); Relationships (4890)]\\n\\nThe community also encodes a primary prevention action: removing polyps (often during colonoscopy) is described as a way to prevent cancer before it starts. This creates a direct prevention linkage from POLYPS to POLYP REMOVAL as an intervention node. [Data: Entities (2669); Relationships (4893)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Adenomas are highlighted as the most common polyp type and a long-term precursor to invasive colon cancer\",\"explanation\":\"Within the set of polyp types, ADENOMA is identified as the most common, explicitly connecting the broad POLYPS entity to a clinically important subtype that is central to malignant progression models. [Data: Entities (2650, 108); Relationships (4887)]\\n\\nAdenomas are further linked to invasive colon cancer through a time-dependent progression statement: adenomas can become invasive colon cancer over many years. This relationship anchors a canonical pathway (adenoma → invasive cancer) used in screening and management rationales. [Data: Entities (4402, 108); Relationships (9235)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Not all polyp subtypes carry the same malignancy risk; hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps are described as rarely becoming cancer\",\"explanation\":\"The community distinguishes polyp subtypes with comparatively low malignant potential. Hyperplastic polyps and inflammatory polyps are both described as rarely turning into cancer, which functions as a risk-stratification signal within the broader “polyps can progress” narrative. [Data: Entities (2667, 2668); Relationships (4891, 4892)]\\n\\nAt the same time, polyps are described as starting as benign growths before potentially transforming, reinforcing that “polyp” is a heterogeneous category: some are low risk, while others (notably adenomas) can be clinically consequential. [Data: Entities (4430, 2650); Relationships (9271)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Familial Adenomatous Polyposis (FAP) is a high-burden inherited driver of numerous polyps, with classic and attenuated forms\",\"explanation\":\"FAP is described as a rare hereditary cancer predisposition syndrome in which affected individuals develop hundreds to thousands of adenomatous polyps in the colon and rectum, tightly coupling inherited risk to a large polyp burden. This creates a strong genetic-risk axis connecting FAP to POLYPS. [Data: Entities (144, 2650); Relationships (9265)]\\n\\nThe community further differentiates FAP into attenuated and classic forms, with attenuated FAP described as a milder form and classic FAP described as the more severe/typical form in which cancer often develops by age 50. This internal taxonomy indicates clinically meaningful heterogeneity within the inherited syndrome node. [Data: Entities (4414, 4431, 144); Relationships (9266, 9268)]\"},{\"summary\":\"A dedicated clinical decision pathway exists for malignant polyps within colon cancer management guidance\",\"explanation\":\"“Treatment for polyps with cancer” is explicitly represented as a distinct colon cancer management topic/section, indicating that malignant polyps are treated as a specific decision pathway rather than being folded into generic non-metastatic/metastatic algorithms. [Data: Entities (4405); Relationships (9218)]\\n\\nBy encoding this as a separate heading/topic linked from COLON CANCER, the community suggests a recognized need for tailored clinical decisions when cancer is found within a polyp (i.e., lesion context matters for management). [Data: Entities (4405); Relationships (9218)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Adenocarcinoma is defined by glandular origin and includes mucus-associated cellular origins in lung contexts\",\"explanation\":\"Adenocarcinoma is broadly characterized as a malignant cancer arising from glandular cells (gland-forming tissue), linking the ADENOCARCINOMA concept directly to GLANDULAR CELLS. This establishes the histologic definition that unifies multiple site-specific adenocarcinoma examples in the community. [Data: Entities (966, 6014); Relationships (13688)]\\n\\nIn lung-specific description, adenocarcinoma is stated to start in mucusmaking cells lining the alveoli and is associated with mucus-secreting lining cells, connecting ADENOCARCINOMA to MUCOUSMAKING CELLS and MUCUS. This adds a mechanistic/phenotypic layer to the histologic definition. [Data: Entities (1067, 966); Relationships (1629, 1483)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Stomach adenocarcinoma subtyping (intestinal vs diffuse) is represented, with intestinal type linked to targeted-therapy-relevant mutations\",\"explanation\":\"The community encodes stomach adenocarcinoma subtypes by linking ADENOCARCINOMA to DIFFUSE TYPE as one of the two major stomach adenocarcinoma types, and by separately defining the INTESTINAL TYPE and its characteristic morphology. [Data: Entities (5456, 5457, 966); Relationships (12127)]\\n\\nIntestinal-type stomach adenocarcinoma is described as having cells that stick together to form tube- and gland-shaped structures, providing a distinguishing structural hallmark. [Data: Entities (5456, 5473); Relationships (12130)]\\n\\nImportantly for treatment impact, intestinal-type adenocarcinoma is described as more likely to have mutations treatable with targeted therapy, creating a direct bridge between subtype classification and therapeutic opportunity (TARGETED THERAPY). [Data: Entities (5456); Relationships (12128)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Procedure eligibility appears as a separate decision node: low-grade adenocarcinoma linked to extrafascial (simple) hysterectomy criteria in a specific setting\",\"explanation\":\"Beyond colon-focused entities, the community includes a procedural eligibility linkage: extrafascial (simple) hysterectomy eligibility criteria include the tumor being a low-grade adenocarcinoma (along with other low-risk features). This encodes how histologic grade/type can gate specific surgical approaches in at least one clinical context. [D